Have you ever been on a hike and spot a beautiful tree, and have no idea what kind of tree it is? Well, as the saying goes….”there’s an app for that”, kinda. Because TREESPNW isn’t a regular app, but a download that doesn’t require cell service, you can use it anywhere anytime. I’ve spent a long time trying to determine what this or that trees is, and this is the only real easy method of identifying trees. The images are clear and show the important features that help to ID the trees. If you are an avid hiker or a beginner, you have probably seen the Sub-Alpine Fir, in the high country. These marvelous trees grow very thin and tall. They cover vast areas in our region. But did you know that the small versions of these trees can grow to be 100+ years old, and not reach your eye level in height. They are called Krummohlz. A one inch diameter Krummholz is about 100 years old. They are the trees that do not bend when you walk to close to them. This download is free and covers the main trees in our region. The app is designed for the west coast of Washington, but covers the many trees in the Inland Northwest. Let me know how you feel about this app/download. And as you hike the high country, hug a tree. Trees give us so much, but nothing is more important than the oxygen we breath On this website, there is a section under GALLERY, called TREES. Check out the many trees of our region. Chic Burge          David Crafton InlandNWRoutes.com

